          "The 4.53% Hispanic population, 24.6% renter rate, and 29.2% college attainment are the district's key demographic tension points. Renters at nearly one-quarter of the population represent a household type with fundamentally different economic stakes than the 79.4% homeowner majority—they are not invested in property tax structures, more vulnerable to housing cost inflation, and historically less aligned with homeowner-dominant Republican coalitions. The growing Hispanic population, while still a minority, reflects broader San Diego County demographic trends that will measurably shift this district's composition over the next decade. The college-attainment rate of 29.2%, while below state averages, includes a meaningful professional and managerial cohort whose policy preferences on education, healthcare access, and fiscal management may not align automatically with traditional conservative positioning. An incumbent who speaks only to homeowner property tax concerns, dismisses renter constituencies as transient, and assumes college-educated professionals will remain reliable Republican voters is building a coalition that is shrinking, not expanding, in real demographic terms.",
