          { name: "Renter & Young-Adult Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 34.6% renter rate in a $88,065 median income district is a substantial constituency—roughly one-third of households—that faces real housing cost and wage-growth constraints distinct from homeowner concerns. The 32-year median age signals a district with significant young-adult population for whom housing affordability, first-time homebuyer access, and career opportunity are direct material concerns. A representative who addresses these constituencies' actual economic constraints—rather than defaulting to homeowner-majority governance around property values and schools—builds political durability against future Democratic organizing and demonstrates responsiveness to one-third of the actual district population." },
          { name: "Minority Community Constituent Attention", tag: "careful", why: "The combined 7% Black, 7% Hispanic, and ~5% demographic gap representing Asian, multiracial, and Native American residents total roughly 14.4% of the district—a meaningful minority whose specific economic and social concerns (housing access, wage equity, healthcare access) can become invisible in a prosperous, white-majority district if the incumbent treats them as settled votes rather than active constituents. In districts this safely Republican, minority communities sometimes become overlooked, creating latent organizing opportunity if a challenger specifically mobilizes around their unaddressed concerns. Visible, specific constituent attention is both a governance obligation and an electoral inoculation." },
          { name: "Ideological Rigidity Over Economic Competence", tag: "avoid", why: "A $88K median income, one-third college-educated, 32-year-old-median constituency evaluates primarily on economic competence, tax fairness, and whether government delivers concrete services—not ideological alignment. An incumbent who campaigns or governs primarily on conservative principle rather than addressing the material economic concerns of prosperous but non-wealthy households, renters, and younger adults risks appearing disconnected from actual constituent priorities. In a district this electorally secure but this economically middle-class, ideological positioning without economic substance is a vulnerability that a credible challenger can exploit." },
          { name: "Taking Homeowner Majority for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "The 65.4% homeownership rate is significant but not overwhelming; it is not a supermajority foundation. An incumbent who consolidates only homeowner interests around property-value protection and school funding while ignoring the renter and younger-adult constituencies' housing and wage concerns is ceding one-third of the district to potential opposition organizing. In a truly safe district this governance approach carries no risk; in an unopposed district where opposition mobilization has simply not occurred, it is a strategic error that presumes permanence where there is only current advantage." }
