          { name: "Healthcare Access & Cost Management for Affluent Households", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $85,307 median household income and 39.7-year median age, SD-14 is a constituency whose healthcare concerns center on cost management, insurance coverage quality, and access to specialized care for aging parents—not emergency safety-net issues. Healthcare policy that demonstrates concrete attention to controlling premium costs, expanding insurance plan choice, and ensuring quality care for middle-class families will reinforce the Republican coalition's economic credibility. Opposition candidates who mobilize on healthcare access or cost concerns risk capturing voters who believe Republican policy is not adequately addressing their family's healthcare economics." },
          { name: "Understanding the 45% Opposition's Specific Drivers", tag: "careful", why: "A 45% opposition result in an affluent, educated, homeowner-dominant district cannot be explained by demographics alone and warrants specific strategic analysis. Whether concentrated among renters (28.9%), college-educated professionals frustrated with education or healthcare policy, younger adults in the demographic gap, or voters whose economic mobility concerns are not addressed by current policy, identifying which constituencies are generating the opposition vote is essential to determining whether 55% is a stable floor or a plurality at risk. Any incumbent who assumes 55% is safe without understanding what generated the 45% will be vulnerable to a challenger who addresses those drivers directly." },
          { name: "Operating as Though 55% Represents a Stable Republican Hold", tag: "avoid", why: "A 55-45 margin in a district where 44.3% are college-educated and voters explicitly evaluate on policy substance is not a landslide that can be managed through base consolidation alone. The opposition's presence in an affluent, educated constituency signals that 45% of the district believes the incumbent or opposition candidate better addresses their economic and social concerns. Any legislator who campaigns this seat as though 55% is a ceiling rather than a working margin—and therefore invests in active constituent engagement on education, healthcare, and economic policy rather than assuming homeowner status ensures Republican dominance—will find the seat more vulnerable than current margins suggest." },
          { name: "Neglecting the ~5.5% Unaccounted Population and Their Specific Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "The demographic gap between reported categories and 100% leaves approximately 5.5% unaccounted for—likely American Indian, Alaska Native, multiracial, or other identity populations whose presence in the district merits specific constituent attention rather than assumption they are absorbed into the white homeowner narrative. These constituencies may have distinct economic concerns, civic participation patterns, or policy priorities that are not visible in aggregate Republican margins but represent latent upside or downside risk depending on whether they feel recognized and served by the incumbent. Neglecting this population in a district where margins are measured in single digits is both a governance failure and a strategic vulnerability." }
