          "Understand and address the 45% opposition's specific drivers: whether concentrated among renters (28.9%) concerned about affordability and economic mobility, college-educated professionals frustrated with healthcare or education policy, or younger adults in the unaccounted demographic gap—identifying which constituencies are generating the opposition vote and offering concrete remedies is more important than assuming 55% is a stable base.",
          "Engage the ~5.5% unaccounted population intentionally: whether American Indian, multiracial, or other identity, this constituency deserves specific constituent attention rather than assumption they are absorbed into the white homeowner majority—their economic concerns and civic participation may represent latent upside for a candidate who acknowledges them directly.",
          "Operate from competitive discipline: a 55-45 margin in an affluent, educated district where voters evaluate on policy substance is not a landslide—it is a working majority that requires active constituent engagement, specific policy delivery on education and healthcare, and genuine responsiveness to the opposition's concerns rather than the assumption that homeowner and college-educated status automatically anchor the seat."
