          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as Evidence of Seat Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "Unopposed election results reflect absence of organized opposition, not absence of electoral vulnerability. An incumbent who interprets six years without a challenger as evidence of durable Republican alignment and demographic security is misreading the strategic environment. If constituent economic concerns are unaddressed, institutional relationships have atrophied, and the opposition is not challenging, it is because no organization has yet materialized — not because one cannot. A challenger who campaigns on concrete economic grievances against an incumbent who has governed with the confidence of demographic safety can rapidly change the seat's competitive status." },
          { name: "Assuming the 23.6% College-Attainment Rate Signals Secure Professional Class Support", tag: "avoid", why: "College attainment at 23.6% indicates educated professionals exist in the district, but does not guarantee their political loyalty or satisfaction with current representation. Healthcare providers, educators, small business owners, and government professionals in moderate-income rural and exurban communities evaluate representation on concrete outcomes: education funding, healthcare access and cost, business climate, and infrastructure quality. An incumbent who has not actively cultivated relationships with these constituencies and cannot point to specific legislative achievements that serve them has allowed that professional class to become a swing group rather than a consolidated base." }
