          { name: "Ignoring the Rural-Capital Divide in Economic Policymaking", tag: "avoid", why: "State capital districts often experience tension between the economic interests of state government (which favors stable funding, employee compensation, and infrastructure investment) and rural constituents (who may prioritize tax limitation, agricultural support, or rural service cost-sharing). Attempting to serve both communities with a single ideological framework—whether favoring state investment or across-the-board austerity—will alienate whichever constituency feels sacrificed. A representative who recognizes that Pierre's state workers and the rural agricultural community have different but equally legitimate economic interests, and who can articulate a coherent framework that addresses both rather than treating them as competing interests, is far more likely to maintain the district's stability and avoid creating an opening for a challenger who can mobilize whichever constituency feels neglected." }
