          "The demographic gap — the four reported categories account for 92.67% of the population, leaving approximately 7.33% unaccounted for — is modest compared to districts with substantial Native American or Hispanic swing constituencies, but not negligible in a district this size. More strategically important is that this is a district where college-educated professionals at high income levels represent a meaningful share of the constituency, and college-educated voters in high-income districts have shown increasing volatility around issues of governance competence, institutional integrity, and alignment with professional-class social values. The 73% result reflects current alignment, but a candidate who assumes this constituency will remain permanently Republican regardless of performance or positioning is misreading the actual vulnerability of affluent, educated districts in an era of partisan fragmentation. The path to defending this seat is not to consolidate the existing 73% more tightly, but to understand what the 27% is expressing and to demonstrate that the Republican offer in SD-25 is substantively better than the alternative on the dimensions that actually matter to high-income homeowners who have options."
