          "SD-HD-13 presents a paradox: it is among Oklahoma's most affluent and educated districts — median household income $91,932, 50.7% college-educated, 86.96% white — yet it produced a 100% Republican result in 2024 because both candidates ran unopposed. This is not a measure of electoral strength; it is the absence of electoral measurement. The district's demographics suggest a stable conservative constituency: older homeowners (median age 39.1), high income, college-educated, low renter rate at 33.5%. These numbers typically correlate with strong Republican performance. However, the unopposed result means there is no baseline data about whether this demographic stability translates into durable voter preference or whether it represents a coalition that could be activated by a credible challenger under different conditions. The strategic question is not whether Republicans can hold SD-HD-13 — the question is whether the current representatives actually know what their constituency wants, because no one has tested it.",
          "The district's wealth and education level are the primary strategic asset and potential vulnerability. A $91,932 median household income places residents in the upper quartile of Oklahoma's economy, and 50.7% college attainment means half the district has completed a four-year degree. This is a constituency with sophisticated policy expectations: they understand tax policy, healthcare economics, education funding mechanisms, and fiscal sustainability. They are not persuaded by partisan rhetoric alone; they evaluate on whether government is delivering effective services and maintaining economic conditions that support their standard of living. The 33.5% renter rate signals a meaningful population of non-homeowners — younger professionals, service-sector workers, or others for whom housing costs may be rising faster than incomes despite the district's overall affluence. A representative who treats SD-HD-13 as secured by demographics and runs a low-engagement campaign risks leaving this educated, affluent constituency without a relationship. When a challenger emerges — and in a district this large and affluent, one eventually will — a representative with no standing relationship to their constituents' actual concerns becomes vulnerable faster than the favorable demographics suggest."
