          "The unaccounted ~5.5% demographic gap — the difference between the four reported categories (87.56% + 5.4% + 1.51% + 0.28% = 94.75%) and 100% — likely represents American Indian/Alaska Native population concentrated in specific precincts or census tracts within the district. While smaller than the AIAN gaps in more rural or tribal-adjacent districts, a 5.5% population is electorally meaningful in a district where the Republican margin is 36-32, and where suburban voters with mixed partisan instincts could be moved by candidates or issues that activate this constituency. The district's 27.5% college attainment and $73,197 income create a constituency that votes based on specific governance performance and policy alignment, not inherited partisanship — which means that any candidate must compete actively on education quality, tax fairness, healthcare access, and responsive local governance, rather than assuming that suburban prosperity automatically translates to Republican loyalty. The Stevens/Auch split, with neither candidate exceeding 36%, reinforces that this district contains multiple competing constituencies with different priorities, and that the winning margin in 2026 will depend on which candidate better consolidates existing support and persuades the opposition's most persuadable voters."
