          "The more immediate strategic vulnerability in SD-HD-28B is the economic profile of its aging, homeowner-dominant constituency. At a 40.6-year median age and only 20.9% college education, this is a district of working-class homeowners for whom income is relatively stable at $67,389 but whose fixed costs — property taxes, healthcare, utilities, home maintenance — are rising faster than wages. The unopposed 2024 victory masks a critical question: would this margin hold if a challenger emerged who spoke directly to healthcare affordability, property tax burden, or rural infrastructure decline? The 19.8% renter rate suggests a smaller but present constituency of younger adults or fixed-income renters for whom housing cost as a percentage of income may be acute. An incumbent who has governed this seat without building an affirmative record on aging homeowner economic security and without demonstrating active engagement with the district's AIAN constituency is a seat that could surprise in a competitive cycle if an opponent offers a credible alternative on these material concerns."
