          "The 6.6% demographic gap — the difference between 93.4% accounted for by the four reported Census categories and 100% — is substantial enough to warrant specific strategic attention. In Oklahoma suburban and exurban districts, this gap typically represents a combination of American Indian/Alaska Native residents, recent immigrants not yet captured in Census reporting, and mixed-race households. At 6.6%, this represents roughly 3,000-4,000 residents depending on district population, a constituency large enough to swing a primary or activate in a general election if engaged with genuine constituent attention. The unopposed 2024 result means no recent electoral data on how these residents have aligned, what concerns activate them, or whether the current Republican incumbent has built authentic relationships with this community. For any challenger, or for an incumbent preparing for future competitive cycles, understanding and building relationships with the 6.6% gap constituency is strategically necessary.",
