          { name: "Treating the 71% Result as a Durable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 71% Republican result achieved through a split between two Republicans (38% and 32%), with no Democratic candidate and an implicit 29% not captured by either winner, is not a demonstration of unified Republican strength — it is a fragmented result that could easily shift if a credible Democratic or independent challenger mobilizes the missing third of the electorate or if the internal Republican divide widens. In a district this homogeneous and wealthy relative to state averages ($66,766 vs. Oklahoma median ~$58k), assuming the 71% is safe rather than contingent on maintaining internal party cohesion and turnout is a strategic error. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns as though 71% guarantees re-election will be blindsided if the opposition consolidates or the internal Republican base splinters further." },
          { name: "Demographic Homogeneity as Strategic Vulnerability Rather Than Strength", tag: "avoid", why: "At 89.3% white and with Hispanic, Asian, Black, and other populations each below 4%, SD-HD-31 is an extremely homogeneous district where nearly all voters share similar racial and ethnic background — a configuration that historically produces stable partisan results but also means there are few natural constituencies to build additional coalition capacity. The absence of meaningful demographic diversity also means that any candidate or party seeking to grow beyond the current 71% must do so through issue-based persuasion of existing demographic groups rather than demographic expansion. Conversely, a candidate who assumes demographic homogeneity equals automatic electability and neglects to build specific relationships with the college-educated, renter, and younger subsets of the white majority will find that homogeneity masks real internal divisions. This district rewards candidates who understand it as multiple constituencies with overlapping interests rather than a monolithic bloc." }
